# SYNOPSIS

```c
vx_instant vx_clock_read(void);
vx_status vx_clock_info_read(vx_clock_info *info);
int64_t vx_clock_utc(void);
vx_status vx_clock_set(vx_handle resource, int64_t utc);
```

# DESCRIPTION

The system has one clock, monotonic, in nanoseconds (`vx_instant`).
Every timestamp and every deadline is on it, and nothing changes it.
It is made from the CPU's cycle counter, the TSC on x86_64 and the virtual counter on aarch64, as the counter times 10^9 divided by the counter's frequency, exactly, so it counts from the counter's zero, about when the machine was reset, not from the kernel's boot.

`vx_clock_read` returns the time on that clock.
It cannot fail.

`vx_clock_info_read` fills <info> with the counter the clock is made from, which sysfs(4) shows as `/sys/clock/info`:

: `counter_hz`
  The counter's frequency, as the kernel calibrated it at boot.
: `flags`
  `VX_CLOCK_INVARIANT` if the counter keeps one rate in every power state, `VX_CLOCK_USER` if user code may read it, `VX_CLOCK_TSC` or `VX_CLOCK_CNTVCT` for which counter it is, and `VX_CLOCK_UTC` once the wall clock has been set.
: `utc_offset`
  The wall clock, as UTC's offset from the monotonic clock: UTC in nanoseconds since 1970 is the monotonic time plus `utc_offset`.
  It is 0 until `VX_CLOCK_UTC`.

User code may always read the counter itself, without a call: `vx_cycles`, in the same header, does it with `rdtsc` or `mrs cntvct_el0`, and `counter_hz` converts what it returns to the clock.

`vx_clock_utc` returns UTC, in nanoseconds since 1970: the monotonic time plus `utc_offset`.
Until the wall clock is set the offset is 0, so it counts from the clock's zero, as do musl's realtime clocks, file times in fsd and tmpfs, and the `realtime` of `/sys/clock/now`, all of which add the same offset.

`vx_clock_set` sets the wall clock to <utc>, nanoseconds since 1970 at the moment of the call: the kernel keeps `utc` less the monotonic time as the offset, and sets `VX_CLOCK_UTC`.
The offset changes in one step; the monotonic clock does not change at all.
<resource> must be the root Resource, with `VX_RIGHT_MANAGE`.
Only devmgr(8) holds it, and it calls `vx_clock_set` when a clock driver reports the time its hardware keeps, as ADR-0031 decides.

The first three make the call `clock_read`, given a pointer to a `vx_clock_info` or none; `vx_clock_set` makes `clock_set`.

# EXAMPLES

The cycle counter, read in user mode and converted to the clock, from `tests/user/proctest.c`:

```c
vx_clock_info clock = {};
CHECK(vx_clock_info_read(&clock) == VX_OK && clock.counter_hz > 1'000'000);
uint64_t c0 = vx_cycles();
nap(10);
uint64_t c1 = vx_cycles();
CHECK(c1 > c0 && (c1 - c0) * 1000 / clock.counter_hz >= 9);
```

